## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to design a practical, testable fair-use layer for a multi-tenant LLM gateway with billing considerations.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant quotas and burst handling using a token bucket
- Isolation of prompts and responses per tenant
- Simple REST API for quota status and consumption
- Observability and a minimal test plan for bucket behavior

## Code Example
```javascript
class TokenBucket {
  constructor(capacity, refillPerMs) {
    this.capacity = capacity;
    this.tokens = capacity;
    this.refillPerMs = refillPerMs;
    this.lastRefill = Date.now();
  }
  tick() {
    const now = Date.now();
    const elapsed = now - this.lastRefill;
    const refill = Math.floor(elapsed * this.refillPerMs);
    if (refill > 0) {
      this.tokens = Math.min(this.capacity, this.tokens + refill);
      this.lastRefill = now;
    }
  }
  tryConsume(n = 1) {
    this.tick();
    if (this.tokens >= n) {
      this.tokens -= n;
      return true;
    }
    return false;
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about lightweight, policy-driven routing in a multi-tenant LLM ops context. Focuses on practical data modeling, decision logic, and test coverage for beginners.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant routing state (budget, privacy)
- Latency estimation and budget checks
- Policy-compliant model selection and auditing
- Basic test plan with reproducible scenarios

## Code Example
```javascript
function routeForTenant(tenant, models, estimates, budgets){
  // pick fastest model within budget and privacy constraints
  const allowed = models.filter(m => tenant.allowedModels.includes(m.name) && m.privacyOK === true);
  const feasible = allowed.filter(m => estimates[m.name] <= tenant.latencyBudgetMs);
  return feasible.sort((a,b) => estimates[a.name]-estimates[b.name])[0] || null;
}
```

